          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Heavenly glory and the manifest presence of God, especially the splendor and transcendence of God's throne.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Exodus 24:10 — beneath the God of Israel is a pavement resembling sapphire, described as clear as the sky.\",\"Ezekiel 1:26 — the divine throne-vision includes a throne appearing like sapphire.\",\"Ezekiel 10:1 — sapphire again characterizes the expanse and throne above the cherubim.\",\"Revelation 21:19 — sapphire forms part of the foundations of the New Jerusalem, reinforcing its association with heavenly glory.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The beauty, preciousness, and restored glory of God's people or holy city.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 54:11 — afflicted Zion is promised foundations laid with sapphires.\",\"Revelation 21:19 — sapphire is among the jeweled foundations of the New Jerusalem.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Truth, faithfulness, purity, or heavenly contemplation, often inferred from sapphire's blue or sky-like appearance.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 24:10 — the sapphire pavement is compared with the clarity of the sky.\",\"Later Christian gemstone, liturgical, and devotional traditions develop these qualities more explicitly than the biblical text itself.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A particular tribe of Israel, based on sapphire's place in the high priest's breastpiece.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 28:18 and 39:11 — sapphire is listed among the breastpiece stones, each associated collectively with Israel's tribes.\",\"Specific stone-to-tribe assignments vary and are not supplied explicitly in the biblical text.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A particular apostle or apostolic virtue, based on the jeweled foundations of the New Jerusalem.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 21:14,19 — the city's foundations bear apostolic names and are adorned with precious stones.\",\"Later interpreters propose differing apostle-to-stone correspondences, with no stable assignment.\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"There is a recognizable primary association with heaven, divine glory, and God's throne, but no single formally settled Christian symbolism. Interpretations vary by biblical commentary, patristic allegory, medieval lapidary tradition, and modern devotional use. The Hebrew sappir may also denote lapis lazuli or a similar blue stone rather than modern mineralogical sapphire.\"}"
        }
      ],
